Biological systems are known to exhibit excellent efficiency in the conversion of molecules and energy management as a result of evolution through millions of years.[1] The overall catalytic performance is governed by the interplay between adjacent functional motifs, where the distance between the corresponding active sites in these motifs is critical, especially for reactions involving multiple steps (Figure 1A).[2] This knowledge has been successfully transferred to the design of biomaterials based on multi-enzyme complexes,[3] where the inter active sites distance was shortened to less than 10 nm to give higher efficiency. In theory, much shorter distance can be obtained in inorganic materials, thus promising to maximize their catalytic efficiency. However, due to the lack of systematic exploration; its correlation to catalytic performance has yet to be established. Unveiling the impact of inter active site distance is likely to release the full potential of inorganic catalysts.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of inorganic porous crystals constructed by linking molecular building blocks periodically,[4] thus are ideally suited for the precise control and progressive tuning of distance between active sites (Figure 1B).
Here, we studied a series of six MOFs based on porphyrin unit, an essential active site for redox reactions in bio-systems.[5] Specifically, MOF-525, PCN-221, PCN-222, PCN-223, PCN-224 and Al-PMOF,[6] were synthesized in pure phase, where each MOF exhibited a distinct profile of distance between porphyrin active sites (Figure 1C). Different from enzymes, where the active sites are far apart due to the packing of auxiliary function groups, the distance between active sites in MOFs are precisely controlled by the dihedral angles at the metal oxide secondary building units (SBUs) (Figure 1C and 2A). In this way, much smaller intervals can be dialled-in, from $12.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ to $21.{\dot{9}}{\dot{\mathsf{A}}}$ , with angstrom level accuracy without facilitation from any auxiliary groups. This allowed us to correlate the distance with the catalytic performance of these MOFs, among which, PCN-223, with the shortest inter active site distance, showed a record high apparent quantum efficiency, $8.0\%$ at $400~\mathsf{n m}$ , for acceptorless dehydrogenation of tetrahydroquinoline (THQ) at room temperature. The catalytic performance of this MOF was even comparable to the best thermal catalysts used for this reaction at elevated temperature $\cdot>$ $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , demonstrating the power of controlling inter active site distance (Table S15).
